# 19. HTTP Headers

HTTP headers provide useful information about web-server behavior.

Example:

```text
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Server: nginx
Content-Type: text/html
Content-Length: 5321
```

Security-related headers may include:

```text
Content-Security-Policy
Strict-Transport-Security
X-Content-Type-Options
Referrer-Policy
Permissions-Policy
```

---

# 20. Server Header

Example:

```text
Server: nginx
```

This can reveal the web-server software.

However:

> **A server header alone does not prove a vulnerability.**

The server may also intentionally hide or modify version information.

---

# 21. Information Disclosure

Information disclosure occurs when an application/server unnecessarily reveals sensitive technical information.

Examples:

```text
Server version
Framework version
Internal paths
Debug information
Stack traces
Internal hostnames
```

Potential impact:

> Attackers may use the information to narrow down possible attack paths.

### Important

Not every information disclosure has the same severity.

Risk depends on:

- What information is revealed
- Whether it enables another attack
- Exposure
- Business impact

---

# 22. HTTP Methods

HTTP methods include:

```text
GET
POST
PUT
PATCH
DELETE
HEAD
OPTIONS
```

An assessment may determine which methods are accepted by a web server.

### Important

An HTTP method being enabled is not automatically a vulnerability.

The security question is:

> **Is the method unnecessarily enabled, improperly authorized, or dangerously configured?**

---

# 23. Security Misconfiguration

Web servers can be incorrectly configured.

Examples:

- Directory listing enabled unnecessarily
- Default files remaining
- Debug information exposed
- Unnecessary HTTP methods
- Weak TLS configuration
- Default configuration
- Unnecessary server information
- Insecure permissions

---

# 24. Directory Listing

A web server may expose a directory listing:

```text
/files/
├── report.pdf
├── backup.zip
├── old-config.txt
└── test/
```

This can unintentionally reveal files.

### Security concern

Users may discover resources that were not intended to be publicly browsable.

### Mitigation

Disable unnecessary directory indexing and ensure sensitive files are not stored in publicly accessible locations.

---

# 25. Default Files

Web servers or applications may contain:

- Installation files
- Test pages
- Example files
- Default administrative pages
- Documentation
- Backup files

These may reveal information or create unnecessary exposure.

### Best Practice

Remove unnecessary files from production environments.

---

# 26. Backup Files

Backup files may accidentally become web-accessible.

Examples:

```text
config.php.bak
database.sql.old
backup.zip
```

Potential concern:

> Backup files can contain source code, configuration information, or sensitive data.

### Prevention

- Keep backups outside the web root where possible.
- Restrict access.
- Use secure backup storage.
- Regularly audit public directories.

---

# 27. TLS/HTTPS

HTTPS protects data in transit using TLS.

Security testing may examine:

- Whether HTTPS is enabled
- Certificate validity
- Redirect behavior
- TLS configuration
- Insecure protocol support

### Important

HTTPS alone does not make an application secure.

It protects communication in transit but does not fix:

- SQL injection
- Broken authorization
- XSS
- Business logic flaws

